    <string name="species_green_fact_1">Grown-ups graze on seagrass and algae. That diet tints the fat under their shell green, which is how they got their name.</string>
    <string name="species_green_fact_2">They can doze underwater for hours, slowing their heartbeat right down to save oxygen.</string>
    <string name="species_green_fact_3">Years later, females swim back to the beach where they hatched to lay eggs of their own.</string>
    <string name="species_loggerhead_fact_1">A big head and strong jaws let it crunch crabs, clams and conch shells.</string>
    <string name="species_loggerhead_fact_2">Young loggerheads drift with ocean currents for years and can cross a whole ocean.</string>
    <string name="species_loggerhead_fact_3">Their shells often carry hitchhikers such as barnacles and tufts of algae.</string>
    <string name="species_hawksbill_fact_1">Its narrow, pointed beak looks like a bird’s and reaches into reef cracks for sponges.</string>
    <string name="species_hawksbill_fact_2">The overlapping plates of its shell give the edge a saw-toothed look.</string>
    <string name="species_hawksbill_fact_3">By nibbling sponges, hawksbills give corals room to grow and keep reefs healthy.</string>
    <string name="species_leatherback_fact_1">The biggest turtle alive. Its shell is tough, leathery skin over tiny bones instead of hard plates.</string>
    <string name="species_leatherback_fact_2">It can dive deeper than a kilometre to hunt jellyfish, its favourite food.</string>
    <string name="species_leatherback_fact_3">Its size and an oily layer under the skin keep it warm in chilly seas.</string>
    <string name="species_kemps_ridley_fact_1">The smallest sea turtle, with a round, grey-green shell.</string>
    <string name="species_kemps_ridley_fact_2">Females come ashore to nest in daylight, often thousands on the same day.</string>
    <string name="species_kemps_ridley_fact_3">Almost all of them nest along one stretch of coast on the Gulf of Mexico.</string>
    <string name="species_olive_ridley_fact_1">Named for the olive colour of its rounded, heart-shaped shell.</string>
    <string name="species_olive_ridley_fact_2">Huge groups nest together over a few nights. This mass arrival is called an arribada.</string>
    <string name="species_olive_ridley_fact_3">It is the most common sea turtle, living in warm seas around the world.</string>
    <string name="species_flatback_fact_1">Its shell is low and flat with turned-up edges, unlike its cousins’ domed shells.</string>
    <string name="species_flatback_fact_2">It nests only in Australia and stays in shallow coastal waters instead of crossing oceans.</string>
    <string name="species_flatback_fact_3">Flatback hatchlings are large for their age, which helps keep them safe.</string>

